FIXME(mundane): You sit at the desk with a borrowed sheet of vellum, a stick of sealing wax, and the candle Devra has set out for you. The letter takes five minutes. Guild standing, formal introduction, a request that the bearer be received and given a hearing. You press the signet ring into the wax. It is the easiest thing you do all night.
# --- COLOR ---
# The signet ring is a plain silver band engraved with the stylized glyph of the Trader's Guild, worn smooth from repeated use.
# The sealing wax is pale yellow and brittle, cracked at the edges from being left out in the dry air.
# The vellum sheet is slightly thicker than standard office paper, with a faint watermark of a coiled serpent in the corner.
# The candle is a stub of beeswax, its wick charred at the tip and surrounded by a pool of congealed residue.
# The desk surface beneath the vellum bears a faint circular impression from a previous candle, slightly darker than the surrounding wood.
# The inkwell beside the vellum contains a deep blue pigment that smells faintly of iron and dried mint.
# --- end ---
# --- FACTUAL ---
# You sit at the desk with a borrowed sheet of vellum, a stick of sealing wax, and the candle Devra has set out for you. The vellum is slightly thicker than standard office paper, with a faint watermark of a coiled serpent in the corner. The inkwell beside it contains a deep blue pigment that smells faintly of iron and dried mint. The signet ring is a plain silver band engraved with the stylized glyph of the Trader's Guild, worn smooth from repeated use. You dip the quill, write the letter in clean lines—guild standing, formal introduction, a request that the bearer be received and given a hearing. The candle is a stub of beeswax, its wick charred at the tip and surrounded by a pool of congealed residue. The sealing wax is pale yellow and brittle, cracked at the edges from being left out in the dry air. You press the signet ring into the wax. It is the easiest thing you do all night. The desk surface beneath the vellum bears a faint circular impression from a previous candle, slightly darker than the surrounding wood.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
# You sit at the desk, the borrowed sheet of vellum before you, its surface slightly thicker than common paper, bearing a faint watermark—a coiled serpent, its form almost imperceptible beneath the faint sheen of the surface. Beside it lies a stick of sealing wax, pale yellow and brittle, cracked at the edges from long exposure to the dry, still air. The candle Devra has set out for you burns low, a stub of beeswax with a wick blackened and charred at the tip, encircled by a pool of congealed residue. The inkwell holds a deep blue pigment, its surface shimmering faintly as though touched by something not quite visible, and it exhales a scent—iron and dried mint, sharp and strangely metallic, as if drawn from some forgotten well beneath the earth. The signet ring rests in your hand: a plain silver band, worn smooth from a thousand touches, engraved with the stylized glyph of the Trader's Guild—its lines worn thin, its meaning buried beneath time. You dip the quill, its tip dark and fine, and begin to write in clean, deliberate lines: a formal introduction, a claim of guild standing, a request that the bearer be received and granted a hearing. The act is simple, mechanical—yet in the silence, the motion feels heavy, as though each stroke draws not ink but memory from a well too deep to name. Beneath the vellum, the desk bears a faint circular impression from a prior candle, its wood darker in that ringed space, as though the wood itself has absorbed the heat and time of a thousand such nights. The wax holds the ring's mark well—clear, sharp, unyielding. It is, in truth, the easiest thing you do all night.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED REH mundane ---
# You sat at the desk, the vellum beneath your fingers—thicker than common parchment, its surface cool and faintly yielding, bearing the watermark of a coiled serpent in the corner, as if the paper itself remembered old secrets. The candle Devra had set out burned low, its beeswax pool congealed like frozen tears around the charred wick. The inkwell beside it held a deep blue pigment that smelled of iron and the sharp, dry breath of mint long withered. You dipped the quill—its tip worn smooth from use—and wrote the letter in clean, deliberate lines: guild standing, formal introduction, a request that the bearer be received and granted a hearing. The signet ring—plain silver, worn smooth by time and a thousand hands—bore the stylized glyph of the Trader's Guild, its edge dull from years of pressing into wax. You pressed it into the cooling wax. The ring sank easily, the impression clear and sharp. It was the easiest thing you had done all night. Beneath the vellum, the desk bore the ghost of a previous candle—a faint, darker circle, a mark left by time and flame, like a scar on the wood.
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC factual ---
# (no findings)
# --- end ---
