FIXME(dread): At first light Devra packs her bag, takes the letter and her oilcloth bundle, and walks out into the clearing dust. She does not say goodbye to Ossal. She does not look back at the wire receiver. The door closes behind her.
# --- COLOR ---
# The oilcloth bundle is wrapped around a stack of transcribed message sheets with handwritten annotations in Devra's cramped script.
# The oilcloth bundle contains a second set of message copies, sealed in a waxed linen pouch that smells faintly of camphor and iron.
# The oilcloth bundle is tied with a frayed cord that matches the one Devra used to secure her personal notebook.
# The brass cylinder on the desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, currently open and unsealed.
# The brazier still holds cold ash and a single charred corner of parchment from a burned transcription.
# The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its indicator light dark, though the terminal port shows a faint residual charge.
# --- end ---
# --- FACTUAL ---
# At first light Devra packs her bag, takes the letter and her oilcloth bundle, and walks out into the clearing dust. The oilcloth bundle is wrapped around a stack of transcribed message sheets with handwritten annotations in Devra's cramped script, tied with a frayed cord that matches the one she used to secure her personal notebook. Inside, a second set of message copies is sealed in a waxed linen pouch that smells faintly of camphor and iron. She does not say goodbye to Ossal. She does not look back at the wire receiver. The brass cylinder on the desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, currently open and unsealed. The brazier still holds cold ash and a single charred corner of parchment from a burned transcription. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its indicator light dark, though the terminal port shows a faint residual charge. The door closes behind her.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED HPL dread ---
# At first light, when the sky wore the pallor of a corpse's skin and the air hung thick with the scent of dust and forgotten things, Devra packed her bag—her hands moving with the precision of one who has long rehearsed the ritual of departure. The letter, brittle and stained with old ink, went inside, sealed with a drop of wax that had dried black as a bruise. The oilcloth bundle, wrapped tight about the stack of transcribed message sheets, was tied with a frayed cord—its thread worn thin as a nerve, just as the one she had once used to bind her personal notebook, that book of secrets too dangerous to keep. Within the bundle, a second set of copies was sealed in a waxed linen pouch, its fabric faintly permeated by the smell of camphor and something older, something like rusted iron and the breath of a tomb. She did not speak to Ossal. She did not look back at the wire receiver, that silent, hollow thing that had once pulsed with the voice of a dead world. The brass cylinder on the desk—Kesharat-issue, stamped with the seal of a vanished order—lay open, its inner mechanism exposed, its seal broken and forgotten. The brazier at the edge of the desk was cold, its ashes still, but one corner of parchment, burned beyond recognition, remained curled like a dead leaf. The terminal port of the receiver still glowed with a faint, dying charge, a last whisper of energy, as if the machine remembered its purpose even as it died. The door closed behind her with a sound like a tomb sealing.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED REH dread ---
# At first light, Devra moved with the quiet precision of one who has already died in spirit. Her bag was packed—no more than a satchel of worn leather, yet heavy with the weight of what it carried. The letter, sealed with a drop of dried wax, lay within like a curse. The oilcloth bundle, wrapped tight with a frayed cord—the same cord she had used to bind her journal, the one she had once sworn never to cut—held the transcribed sheets, their margins crawling with her cramped, desperate script. A second cache, sealed in waxed linen, lay hidden within: message copies, their edges brittle, their scent faintly of camphor and iron, as though they had been drawn from a tomb. She did not look back. Ossal was not there to see her go. The wire receiver stood silent on the desk, its indicator dark, its hollow eye unseeing. The brass cylinder—Kesharat's own cipher seal—gaped open, its inner mechanism exposed, like a wound. In the brazier, cold ash cradled the last ghost of a parchment, a single corner still charred, black as a dying star. The terminal port still hummed with a faint, dying charge—enough to wake a dead man's memory, but not enough to speak. The door closed behind her with a sound like a tomb sealing.
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC factual ---
