FIXME(dread): Ossal comes out of the back room a few minutes later. He sees the empty desk. He sits down at it. He picks up a transcription pencil and sets it down. He picks it up again. He is an inspector, not an operator. His transcriptions will be slow and they will have errors. The settlements downstream will receive garbled or delayed messages for weeks. Some of those messages are medical requests. Some of them are supply orders. Some of them are, perhaps, instructions from something that is not human. All of them will be late.
# --- COLOR ---
# The transcription pencil on Ossal's desk is a standard-issue Kesharat number 7, its wood worn down to the lead in the grip.
# The brazier in the corner holds cold ash and a single charred tea leaf.
# The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its brass cylinder unmarked by fingerprints.
# The official file of transcriptions is open to a page dated three days prior, with a handwritten note in Devra's hand: "Still no origin header."
# The back room door is ajar, with a shadow cast across the floor that does not match the brazier's light.
# A single sheet of paper lies face-down on the desk, its corner bent as if recently lifted.
# --- end ---
# --- FACTUAL ---
# Ossal comes out of the back room a few minutes later. The door is ajar, casting a shadow across the floor that does not match the brazier's light. He sees the empty desk. The transcription pencil on his desk is a standard-issue Kesharat number 7, its wood worn down to the lead in the grip. He sits down at it. He picks up the pencil and sets it down. He picks it up again. The brazier in the corner holds cold ash and a single charred tea leaf. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its brass cylinder unmarked by fingerprints. The official file of transcriptions is open to a page dated three days prior, with a handwritten note in Devra's hand: "Still no origin header." A single sheet of paper lies face-down on the desk, its corner bent as if recently lifted. He is an inspector, not an operator. His transcriptions will be slow and they will have errors. The settlements downstream will receive garbled or delayed messages for weeks. Some of those messages are medical requests. Some of them are supply orders. Some of them are, perhaps, instructions from something that is not human. All of them will be late.
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED HPL dread ---
# Ossal emerges from the back room a few minutes later, his shadow stretching unnaturally across the floor—long, thin, and misshapen, as though cast not by the brazier's dim glow, but by some deeper, unseen source. The door remains ajar, and the line of darkness it casts does not align with the flame's angle, nor with the geometry of the room. He sees the empty desk—void of the usual clutter, yet more disturbing for its stillness. The transcription pencil lies where it was left: a standard-issue Kesharat number 7, its wood worn smooth and darkened by pressure, the lead exposed to the bone-white nub of a point that has long since ceased to write. He sits, his chair creaking with a sound too old to be natural. He lifts the pencil, holds it for a moment as though weighing its substance, then sets it down. He picks it up again. His fingers tremble, though not from cold. The brazier in the corner holds only cold ash, and within it, a single charred tea leaf—its form twisted beyond recognition, as though it had burned not in fire, but in some other, older heat. The wire receiver on the desk is silent, its brass cylinder untouched, devoid of fingerprints, as though no hand had ever rested upon it. The official file of transcriptions lies open to a page dated three days prior—the ink faint, the script precise, but the note in Devra's hand—"Still no origin header"—is written in a hand that does not belong to her. He knows that. He has seen her handwriting before, and this is not it. A single sheet of paper lies face-down on the desk, its corner bent as if recently lifted, though no wind has stirred the air. The silence is not empty. It is full of waiting. He is an inspector, not an operator. His transcriptions will be slow, hesitant, and inevitably flawed. The settlements downstream will receive garbled, delayed messages—weeks late, or not at all. Some will be medical requests, the names of the dying, the pleas of children with fevered lungs. Some will be supply orders—grain, medicine, fuel. And some—oh, some will be instructions. Not from men. Not from any known station. From something that is not human, that has never spoken, that has never
# --- end ---
# --- VOICED REH dread ---
# Ossal emerged from the back room a moment later, the door ajar, its shadow stretching across the floor—wrong, too long, not cast by the brazier's dim glow. His eyes fixed on the empty desk. The transcription pencil lay there, a standard Kesharat number 7, its wood worn thin to the lead, the grip worn smooth by countless hands. He sat. He picked up the pencil. Set it down. Picked it up again, as if the weight of it might bear some answer. The brazier in the corner held only cold ash and a single blackened tea leaf, curling like a dead thing in the dust. The wire receiver on the desk was silent, its brass cylinder untouched, unmarked—no fingerprint, no sign of life. The official file of transcriptions lay open to a page dated three days past, and there, in Devra's hand, a single note: *Still no origin header.* A single sheet of paper lay face-down, its corner bent, as though recently lifted by fingers that should not have been there. He was an inspector, not an operator. His transcriptions would be slow, clumsy, flawed. And the settlements downstream—those distant, starving outposts—would receive their messages late, garbled, broken. Some would carry pleas for medicine. Others, orders for food, for fuel, for tools. And some—perhaps—messages not from men, not from any living thing, but from that which waits beyond the silence, beyond the wires, beyond the edge of what the mind can name. And none of them would come in time.
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC factual ---
# (no findings)
# --- end ---
# --- CRITIC voiced HPL dread ---
