    # You shoulder your pack and walk out into the cleared morning. The mesa is wide and quiet under a thin red sun. The pennant on the pole has gone slack. The brass cylinder on her desk is a Kesharat-issue cipher seal, its surface worn smooth from repeated handling. The wire receiver on the desk is cold to the touch and shows no residual warmth from recent use. The cup of tea on the brazier is empty, its dregs dark and crystallized at the bottom. The pot of tea on the brazier is still warm, its surface faintly steamed despite the cold morning air. The ledger on the desk bears a fresh entry in Devra's hand, dated the night before, marked with a red wax seal. The signal tower on the roof is still silent, its brass fittings dull and unpolished under the red sun. The dust mask hanging on the wall is missing one strap, its fabric frayed from repeated use. Whatever happens next at this relay, it is for Ossal and the wire to settle between them.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED HPL mundane ---
    # You shoulder your pack and stride out into the cleared morning, where the mesa stretches wide and silent beneath a thin, blood-red sun that casts no true shadow. The pennant upon the pole hangs limp, lifeless, as though even the wind had forgotten to stir it. On the desk, the brass cylinder—Kesharat-issue, its surface worn smooth by countless hands—lies inert, its engravings dimmed by time and use. The wire receiver is cold to the touch, devoid of warmth, as though no human hand had touched it in hours. The cup of tea upon the brazier is empty, its dregs congealed into a dark, crystalline residue at the bottom. Yet the pot beside it still breathes faint steam, its surface shimmering with a heat that defies the chill of the morning. The ledger on the desk bears a fresh entry in Devra's hand—written the night before—and sealed with a red wax mark, still soft and unbroken. The signal tower upon the roof remains mute, its brass fittings dull and unpolished, untouched by any recent hand. The dust mask hanging on the wall is missing one strap, its fabric frayed and threadbare from repeated use. And now, as the silence deepens, you feel it—the weight of what is to come. Whatever unfolds at this relay, it is for Ossal and the wire to settle between them, in a silence older than memory.
    # --- end ---
    # --- VOICED REH mundane ---
    # You heave the pack onto your shoulders and step out into the clearing morning. The mesa stretches wide and silent beneath a thin, blood-red sun, its sands bleached pale by the cold light. The pennant on the pole hangs limp, dead as a corpse's breath. On the desk, the brass cylinder of the Kesharat cipher seal lies worn smooth by the grip of a thousand hands, a relic of secrets traded in the dark. The wire receiver is cold as a tombstone, no warmth lingering from recent use. The tea cup on the brazier is empty, its dregs black and crystallized at the bottom like old blood. Yet the teapot beside it still breathes faint steam, a ghost of warmth in the frigid air. The ledger bears a fresh entry in Devra's hand—dated to the night before—sealed with a red wax that glistens like dried fire. The signal tower on the roof stands mute, its brass fittings dull and unpolished under the red sun. The dust mask on the wall is missing one strap, its fabric frayed and thin from a hundred uses. Whatever comes now, it is for Ossal and the wire to settle between them—no man's hand, no voice, no prayer, can alter what the wire will carry.
    # --- end ---
